sandbox: allow fcntl(F_DUPFD_QUERY) instead of killing the process

Mesa >= 25.3 uses fcntl(fd1, F_DUPFD_QUERY, fd2) in os_same_file_description()
to test whether two DRM fds share an open file description. Under X11 the GPU
process holds two separate descriptions for the same DRM node -- ozone's
GBMSupportX11 dup()s its own DRI3 fd for the gbm device, while libEGL's DRI3
loader holds another -- so Mesa's pipe-screen cache runs that comparison while
creating a WebGL context.

F_DUPFD_QUERY is not in RestrictFcntlCommands()' allowlist, so it reaches
.Default(CrashSIGSYS()) and the GPU process is killed. The renderer then fails
with "BindToCurrentSequence failed" and getContext("webgl") returns null:
WebGL is dead on any X11 desktop with Mesa >= 25.3 and Linux >= 6.10.

Mesa has a correct fallback chain (kcmp, then an epoll trick, then a stat
comparison in u_screen.c) for every errno the kernel can return, but a fatal
trap never returns, so none of it is reachable. Chromium already relies on that
fallback for the same Mesa function by answering kcmp with Error(ENOSYS).

Allowing the command is safe: f_dupfd_query() is a one-bit equality test over
two descriptors already in the caller's own fd table, with no capability check,
no cross-process reach and no pointer ordering (unlike kcmp, which is withheld
for that reason). The kernel lists it in check_fcntl_cmd() among the commands
permitted on an O_PATH descriptor, alongside F_DUPFD, F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC, F_GETFD,
F_SETFD and F_GETFL -- all already allowed here. The same oracle is in any case
constructible from F_SETFL/F_GETFL, which are allowed.

Firefox fixed this the same way in the browser (Mozilla #1995035), as did
Ladybird (#10015).

Note for forward-porting: the allowlist must be amended in
RestrictFcntlCommands(), not in bpf_gpu_policy_linux.cc, which only handles
__NR_fcntl. On 32-bit ARM the call arrives as __NR_fcntl64 and baseline_policy.cc
routes it straight to this helper, so a GPU-policy-only change misses armhf.
--- a/sandbox/linux/seccomp-bpf-helpers/syscall_parameters_restrictions.cc
+++ b/sandbox/linux/seccomp-bpf-helpers/syscall_parameters_restrictions.cc
@@ -66,6 +66,25 @@
 
 #endif  // BUILDFLAG(IS_ANDROID)
 
+// F_DUPFD_QUERY was added in Linux 6.10 and may be missing from the build
+// sysroot's headers. Mesa (>= 25.3) uses it in os_same_file_description() to
+// test whether two DRM fds share an open file description.
+//
+// <linux/fcntl.h> cannot be included here to pick up the definition, because
+// its struct flock and struct f_owner_ex collide with the ones glibc's
+// <fcntl.h> already declared.
+#if !defined(F_DUPFD_QUERY)
+#define F_DUPFD_QUERY 1027  // F_LINUX_SPECIFIC_BASE + 3
+#endif
+// glibc does not expose F_LINUX_SPECIFIC_BASE, but it does expose
+// F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC, which the kernel defines as that base + 6. Tie the value
+// above to it so a platform that disagreed would fail the build rather than
+// silently allow the wrong fcntl() command.
+#if defined(F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC)
+static_assert(F_DUPFD_QUERY == F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC - 3,
+              "F_DUPFD_QUERY does not match the platform's fcntl.h");
+#endif
+
 #if defined(__arm__) && !defined(MAP_STACK)
 #define MAP_STACK 0x20000  // Daisy build environment has old headers.
 #endif
@@ -329,6 +348,7 @@ ResultExpr RestrictFcntlCommands() {
               F_SETLKW,
               F_GETLK,
               F_DUPFD,
+              F_DUPFD_QUERY,
               F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC
 #if defined(__powerpc64__)
 // On PPC64, F_SETLK, F_GETLK, F_SETLKW are defined as the 64-bit variants
--- a/sandbox/linux/seccomp-bpf-helpers/syscall_parameters_restrictions.h
+++ b/sandbox/linux/seccomp-bpf-helpers/syscall_parameters_restrictions.h
@@ -52,8 +52,8 @@ SANDBOX_EXPORT bpf_dsl::ResultExpr Restr
 SANDBOX_EXPORT bpf_dsl::ResultExpr RestrictMprotectFlags();
 
 // Restrict fcntl(2) cmd argument to:
-// We allow F_GETFL, F_SETFL, F_GETFD, F_SETFD, F_DUPFD, F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC,
-// F_SETLK, F_SETLKW and F_GETLK.
+// We allow F_GETFL, F_SETFL, F_GETFD, F_SETFD, F_DUPFD, F_DUPFD_QUERY,
+// F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC, F_SETLK, F_SETLKW and F_GETLK.
 // Also, in F_SETFL, restrict the allowed flags to: O_ACCMODE | O_APPEND |
 // O_NONBLOCK | O_SYNC | O_LARGEFILE | O_CLOEXEC | O_NOATIME.
 SANDBOX_EXPORT bpf_dsl::ResultExpr RestrictFcntlCommands();
